  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Guidance is met in FY26 ($1.75B revenue) and VRSN takes the .com escalator in each of the four permitted years from Nov-26 (as it did in 2021-24), with .net at +10% from Mar-27. The AI-driven registration surge (new units +21% YoY in Q2-26, domain base +5.2-6.0% guided) fades as the large late-2025/2026 cohorts hit first-time renewal at mid-40% rates, leaving the base growing ~1-2% a year. Revenue compounds ~8% through FY30, then slows to ~4-5% when the next six-year window opens with two price-freeze years. Margins expand ~3pp on a fixed cost base; the business stays an annuity with near-zero reinvestment needs.",
    "bull": "AI-enabled business and website formation proves structural rather than a cohort spike: domain base growth holds ~3% a year while the full 7% .com escalator is taken every permitted year, .web launches with uncapped pricing and adds a modest new stream, and the 2030 .com renewal preserves the escalator. Revenue compounds ~9-10% through FY30 and 6% thereafter, adjusted EBITDA margin reaches 79%, and the market keeps paying ~22x for a contract-protected annuity.",
    "bear": "The single-contract dependency bites: the Department of Commerce/NTIA or ICANN, under political pressure on .com pricing, blocks or curtails the escalator after the Nov-26 increase (or the 2030 renewal removes it), while the first-time-renewal air pocket, new gTLD/ccTLD substitution (.ai/.io/.co) and app/social/AI-agent navigation shrink the .com base. Revenue growth falls to 2-3% a year, margins drift down a point as costs keep rising, and the exit multiple compresses to 15x as the market re-rates VRSN from compounder to capped utility."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "VRSN is valued as an unlevered owner-FCF annuity: SBC (including RSU tax-withholding repurchases) is expensed and the share count held flat, so the ~$0.9-1.2B/yr of buybacks is the mechanism keeping the count flat rather than an extra source of value. The equity is the DCF enterprise value less ~$1.3B of net debt. The key value drivers are the .com price escalator (4 of every 6 years at up to 7%), registration volume, and whether the contract terms survive the 2030 renewal.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Regulatory: Commerce/NTIA or ICANN curtails .com pricing flexibility, or the 2030 renewal removes it (bear case).",
      "Volume: first-time renewal of the large 2025-26 AI-era cohorts at mid-40% rates creates a 2H26-2027 domain-base air pocket.",
      "Substitution: new gTLDs/ccTLDs and app/social/AI-agent navigation erode .com's share of new businesses over the decade.",
      "Terminal dependence: the terminal value is most of EV; a 1pp WACC change moves value by roughly 15-20%.",
      "Unverified inputs: the $10.97 .com fee from 1-Nov-26 and the raised FY26 guide come from the Analysis JSON (Q2-26 call), not from an 8-K seen in this build."
    ]
  },
